The POST message here,
it's the POST annotation.

00:32:58.080 --> 00:33:04.740
And you get a JSON body
out of that POST message.

00:33:04.740 --> 00:33:08.640
And here, it's the model
from API SDK, which is

00:33:08.640 --> 00:33:10.920
provided by the NuGet package.

00:33:10.920 --> 00:33:13.810
And that model is
then in a form,

00:33:13.810 --> 00:33:15.880
which you can process easily.

00:33:15.880 --> 00:33:19.140
And here I have added
some if/else switches.

00:33:19.140 --> 00:33:20.930
You can also use a
switch statement.

00:33:20.930 --> 00:33:22.020
Doesn't matter.

00:33:22.020 --> 00:33:26.550
And how to test it now
locally on your machine

00:33:26.550 --> 00:33:29.460
before deploying it in the
cloud and connecting it directly

00:33:29.460 --> 00:33:31.530
to the chatbot.

00:33:31.530 --> 00:33:38.260
So I use here now the debug
mode locally on my laptop.

00:33:38.260 --> 00:33:43.990
And use Postman or you can
use any other REST client.

00:33:43.990 --> 00:33:47.380
Paste the JSON in you
have formerly copied out

00:33:47.380 --> 00:33:49.570
of the API.AI console.

00:33:49.570 --> 00:33:53.950
And then fire that
POST to the local host.

00:33:53.950 --> 00:33:55.190
You can see it here.

00:33:55.190 --> 00:33:58.300
It's now my local WebHook.

00:33:58.300 --> 00:34:00.920
And [INAUDIBLE] back
to Visual Studio.

00:34:00.920 --> 00:34:05.615
Hopefully, yeah, it holds on the
line where the activate holiday

00:34:05.615 --> 00:34:08.050
mode intent is processed.

00:34:08.050 --> 00:34:11.530
And the action name was
called activate holiday mode.

00:34:11.530 --> 00:34:15.880
And now I have to read out the
parameter, so the date period.

00:34:15.880 --> 00:34:18.320
And you have your
[INAUDIBLE] structure.

00:34:18.320 --> 00:34:19.659
It's well-formed.

00:34:19.659 --> 00:34:21.429
And you have the parameters.

00:34:21.429 --> 00:34:24.100
And you see, OK,
yeah it's very small.

00:34:24.100 --> 00:34:26.320
But you have one parameter.

00:34:26.320 --> 00:34:28.090
It's named date period.

00:34:28.090 --> 00:34:30.159
And it has that value.

00:34:30.159 --> 00:34:32.469
And you can fetch
out that key value

00:34:32.469 --> 00:34:36.190
pair out of that structure.

00:34:36.190 --> 00:34:38.080
And then I call--

00:34:38.080 --> 00:34:40.179
I have a API
Manager class, which

00:34:40.179 --> 00:34:43.060
separates all our
domain-specific API away

00:34:43.060 --> 00:34:44.469
from the controller.

00:34:44.469 --> 00:34:46.510
Then I call the
set holiday mode,

00:34:46.510 --> 00:34:50.650
activate it, and
activate the date period.

00:34:50.650 --> 00:34:53.469
So that's the
domain-specific API call.

00:34:53.469 --> 00:34:55.150
And then I prepare
the fulfillment.

00:34:55.150 --> 00:34:57.220
And the fulfillment
is the response

00:34:57.220 --> 00:35:00.800
of the chatbot to the user.

00:35:00.800 --> 00:35:07.360
And here I do it mostly like
then before in the console.

00:35:07.360 --> 00:35:12.790
I replace a placeholder
key with the date period

00:35:12.790 --> 00:35:14.830
the user was expected.

00:35:14.830 --> 00:35:16.560
And then I return OK.

00:35:16.560 --> 00:35:19.090
So HTTP 200.

00:35:19.090 --> 00:35:21.880
And if I go back to
the Postman, then you

00:35:21.880 --> 00:35:25.660
can see here the fulfillment,
which is also JSON,

00:35:25.660 --> 00:35:29.450
and which is sent back later on
if you deploy it to the cloud

00:35:29.450 --> 00:35:31.650
